  user_input {
    user_response: "## CRITICAL VISUAL CORRECTION \342\200\224 CINEMATIC BACKGROUND IS NOT ACTUALLY VISIBLE\n\nThe current implementation claims to have a cinematic broadcast background system, but the actual rendered website does NOT visually communicate this strongly enough.\n\nDo not simply describe the background as cinematic in code.\n\nI need the background imagery to be **visibly present in the actual rendered experience**.\n\n### 1. HERO \342\200\224 REAL BROADCAST PRODUCTION IMAGE\n\nThe hero must have a high-quality cinematic background image showing an authentic professional broadcast environment.\n\nUse imagery such as:\n\n* Professional broadcast camera operator\n* Multi-camera live production\n* Concert stage with LED wall\n* Corporate keynote being professionally filmed\n* Broadcast control room\n* Vision mixing / production desk\n* Technical crew working behind the scenes\n\nThe image should feel like a real production company working on a serious international event.\n\nIt must NOT look like:\n\n* AI-generated technology artwork\n* Generic startup imagery\n* Futuristic sci-fi\n* Abstract gradients\n* Neon cyberpunk\n* 3D renders\n* Generic stock-business photography\n\n### 2. IMAGE MUST ACTUALLY BE VISIBLE\n\nDo NOT hide the photography underneath an opaque black background.\n\nThe image should remain clearly recognizable.\n\nUse:\n\nIMAGE\n\342\206\222 dark cinematic overlay\n\342\206\222 subtle vignette\n\342\206\222 readable typography\n\nThe final viewer should immediately be able to tell:\n\n**\342\200\234This company works in professional live broadcast production.\342\200\235**\n\nThe photography should occupy a substantial portion of the hero rather than being an invisible background layer.\n\n### 3. CINEMATIC TREATMENT\n\nThe background should feel premium and cinematic through:\n\n* Deep blacks\n* Graphite\n* Dark charcoal\n* Natural shadows\n* Controlled highlights\n* Subtle film grain\n* Soft vignette\n* Atmospheric depth\n* Strong contrast\n* Professional color grading\n\nDo NOT create artificial glowing effects.\n\nThe sophistication should come from **photography, lighting, composition and typography**.\n\n### 4. ADD CINEMATIC IMAGERY THROUGH THE PAGE\n\nDo not limit the photography to the hero.\n\nIntroduce carefully selected full-width or large-format photography into major sections such as:\n\n**CAPABILITIES**\n\nShow real production equipment, cameras, vision mixing and technical crews.\n\n**PRODUCTION IN ACTION**\n\nUse large cinematic imagery of live events, stages, audiences, cameras and production teams.\n\n**GLOBAL PRODUCTION**\n\nUse an authentic international-event production image.\n\n**FINAL CTA**\n\nUse a subtle cinematic production image behind or alongside the CTA.\n\nThe page should feel like a visual journey through the world of professional broadcast production.\n\n### 5. IMAGE HIERARCHY\n\nDo not put an image behind every section.\n\nMaintain restraint.\n\nUse photography strategically:\n\nHero \342\206\222 strongest cinematic image\n\nCapabilities \342\206\222 supporting production imagery\n\nProduction in Action \342\206\222 large visual moment\n\nGlobal Production \342\206\222 atmospheric image\n\nFinal CTA \342\206\222 subtle image\n\nOther sections \342\206\222 clean dark/graphite surfaces\n\nThis prevents the site from becoming visually noisy.\n\n### 6. IMPORTANT \342\200\224 NO FAKE PORTFOLIO CLAIMS\n\nIf professional imagery is temporary stock/reference photography, DO NOT label it:\n\n\342\200\234D1 Streams Project\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Client: XYZ\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Case Study\342\200\235\n\nor imply that D1 Streams produced the event.\n\nThe photography is being used strictly to establish the **visual atmosphere and production category**.\n\nDo not invent clients, statistics, testimonials or event credits.\n\n### 7. BACKGROUND DEPTH\n\nThe site should NOT look like:\n\nblack background + white text + occasional image.\n\nInstead, create a layered visual system:\n\n* Cinematic photography\n* Near-black surfaces\n* Graphite sections\n* Dark charcoal transitions\n* Subtle grain\n* Natural image shadows\n* Controlled vignettes\n* Strong typographic hierarchy\n\nThe transitions between sections should feel intentional and editorial.\n\n### 8. MOBILE\n\nThe cinematic imagery must remain visible on mobile.\n\nDo not simply remove all background photography on mobile.\n\nInstead:\n\n* Use appropriately cropped images\n* Use responsive image sources\n* Adjust focal position\n* Increase overlay only where necessary for readability\n* Use smaller optimized images\n* Avoid loading unnecessarily large desktop assets\n\n### 9. PERFORMANCE\n\nThe cinematic background must NOT make the website slow.\n\nUse:\n\n* WebP/AVIF\n* Responsive image sizes\n* `srcset`\n* Lazy loading for below-fold imagery\n* Optimized hero image\n* Proper image dimensions\n* Compressed assets\n\nDo NOT load enormous 4K images when a smaller source provides the same visual quality.\n\nIf video is used, it must be optional and must never block the initial render.\n\nA high-quality static cinematic image is preferable to a heavy video if necessary.\n\n### 10. FINAL VISUAL TEST\n\nBefore considering this task complete, inspect the actual rendered website.\n\nAsk:\n\n> If I hide the text, does the visual environment itself communicate professional live broadcast production?\n\nIf the answer is no, the background implementation is NOT complete.\n\nThe final website should immediately communicate:\n\n**PRODUCTION. SCALE. PRECISION. EXPERIENCE.**\n\nIt should feel like a serious international broadcast production company \342\200\224 not a generic technology startup.\n\nKeep the existing:\n\n* Syne typography\n* Plus Jakarta Sans\n* JetBrains Mono technical labels\n* D1 Streams logo\n* Plain-text NAMACO Technology credit\n* Responsive architecture\n* Performance optimizations\n\nDo not redesign those elements.\n\nThis task is specifically to make the **cinematic broadcast photography and visual background atmosphere visibly present and unmistakable throughout the experience.**"
    items {
      text: "## CRITICAL VISUAL CORRECTION \342\200\224 CINEMATIC BACKGROUND IS NOT ACTUALLY VISIBLE\n\nThe current implementation claims to have a cinematic broadcast background system, but the actual rendered website does NOT visually communicate this strongly enough.\n\nDo not simply describe the background as cinematic in code.\n\nI need the background imagery to be **visibly present in the actual rendered experience**.\n\n### 1. HERO \342\200\224 REAL BROADCAST PRODUCTION IMAGE\n\nThe hero must have a high-quality cinematic background image showing an authentic professional broadcast environment.\n\nUse imagery such as:\n\n* Professional broadcast camera operator\n* Multi-camera live production\n* Concert stage with LED wall\n* Corporate keynote being professionally filmed\n* Broadcast control room\n* Vision mixing / production desk\n* Technical crew working behind the scenes\n\nThe image should feel like a real production company working on a serious international event.\n\nIt must NOT look like:\n\n* AI-generated technology artwork\n* Generic startup imagery\n* Futuristic sci-fi\n* Abstract gradients\n* Neon cyberpunk\n* 3D renders\n* Generic stock-business photography\n\n### 2. IMAGE MUST ACTUALLY BE VISIBLE\n\nDo NOT hide the photography underneath an opaque black background.\n\nThe image should remain clearly recognizable.\n\nUse:\n\nIMAGE\n\342\206\222 dark cinematic overlay\n\342\206\222 subtle vignette\n\342\206\222 readable typography\n\nThe final viewer should immediately be able to tell:\n\n**\342\200\234This company works in professional live broadcast production.\342\200\235**\n\nThe photography should occupy a substantial portion of the hero rather than being an invisible background layer.\n\n### 3. CINEMATIC TREATMENT\n\nThe background should feel premium and cinematic through:\n\n* Deep blacks\n* Graphite\n* Dark charcoal\n* Natural shadows\n* Controlled highlights\n* Subtle film grain\n* Soft vignette\n* Atmospheric depth\n* Strong contrast\n* Professional color grading\n\nDo NOT create artificial glowing effects.\n\nThe sophistication should come from **photography, lighting, composition and typography**.\n\n### 4. ADD CINEMATIC IMAGERY THROUGH THE PAGE\n\nDo not limit the photography to the hero.\n\nIntroduce carefully selected full-width or large-format photography into major sections such as:\n\n**CAPABILITIES**\n\nShow real production equipment, cameras, vision mixing and technical crews.\n\n**PRODUCTION IN ACTION**\n\nUse large cinematic imagery of live events, stages, audiences, cameras and production teams.\n\n**GLOBAL PRODUCTION**\n\nUse an authentic international-event production image.\n\n**FINAL CTA**\n\nUse a subtle cinematic production image behind or alongside the CTA.\n\nThe page should feel like a visual journey through the world of professional broadcast production.\n\n### 5. IMAGE HIERARCHY\n\nDo not put an image behind every section.\n\nMaintain restraint.\n\nUse photography strategically:\n\nHero \342\206\222 strongest cinematic image\n\nCapabilities \342\206\222 supporting production imagery\n\nProduction in Action \342\206\222 large visual moment\n\nGlobal Production \342\206\222 atmospheric image\n\nFinal CTA \342\206\222 subtle image\n\nOther sections \342\206\222 clean dark/graphite surfaces\n\nThis prevents the site from becoming visually noisy.\n\n### 6. IMPORTANT \342\200\224 NO FAKE PORTFOLIO CLAIMS\n\nIf professional imagery is temporary stock/reference photography, DO NOT label it:\n\n\342\200\234D1 Streams Project\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Client: XYZ\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Case Study\342\200\235\n\nor imply that D1 Streams produced the event.\n\nThe photography is being used strictly to establish the **visual atmosphere and production category**.\n\nDo not invent clients, statistics, testimonials or event credits.\n\n### 7. BACKGROUND DEPTH\n\nThe site should NOT look like:\n\nblack background + white text + occasional image.\n\nInstead, create a layered visual system:\n\n* Cinematic photography\n* Near-black surfaces\n* Graphite sections\n* Dark charcoal transitions\n* Subtle grain\n* Natural image shadows\n* Controlled vignettes\n* Strong typographic hierarchy\n\nThe transitions between sections should feel intentional and editorial.\n\n### 8. MOBILE\n\nThe cinematic imagery must remain visible on mobile.\n\nDo not simply remove all background photography on mobile.\n\nInstead:\n\n* Use appropriately cropped images\n* Use responsive image sources\n* Adjust focal position\n* Increase overlay only where necessary for readability\n* Use smaller optimized images\n* Avoid loading unnecessarily large desktop assets\n\n### 9. PERFORMANCE\n\nThe cinematic background must NOT make the website slow.\n\nUse:\n\n* WebP/AVIF\n* Responsive image sizes\n* `srcset`\n* Lazy loading for below-fold imagery\n* Optimized hero image\n* Proper image dimensions\n* Compressed assets\n\nDo NOT load enormous 4K images when a smaller source provides the same visual quality.\n\nIf video is used, it must be optional and must never block the initial render.\n\nA high-quality static cinematic image is preferable to a heavy video if necessary.\n\n### 10. FINAL VISUAL TEST\n\nBefore considering this task complete, inspect the actual rendered website.\n\nAsk:\n\n> If I hide the text, does the visual environment itself communicate professional live broadcast production?\n\nIf the answer is no, the background implementation is NOT complete.\n\nThe final website should immediately communicate:\n\n**PRODUCTION. SCALE. PRECISION. EXPERIENCE.**\n\nIt should feel like a serious international broadcast production company \342\200\224 not a generic technology startup.\n\nKeep the existing:\n\n* Syne typography\n* Plus Jakarta Sans\n* JetBrains Mono technical labels\n* D1 Streams logo\n* Plain-text NAMACO Technology credit\n* Responsive architecture\n* Performance optimizations\n\nDo not redesign those elements.\n\nThis task is specifically to make the **cinematic broadcast photography and visual background atmosphere visibly present and unmistakable throughout the experience.**"
