  user_input {
    user_response: "## VISUAL REFINEMENT \342\200\224 MAKE THE WEBSITE LUXURIOUS, NOT TOO DARK\n\nThe current background treatment is too dark and is beginning to feel flat, heavy and slightly boring.\n\nDo NOT abandon the cinematic broadcast direction. Instead, elevate it into a **luxury international media-production aesthetic**.\n\nThe goal is:\n\n**CINEMATIC + LUXURIOUS + EDITORIAL + PROFESSIONAL + TECHNICAL**\n\nNot:\n\n**BLACK + DARK + BLACK + DARK**\n\n### 1. CREATE A RICHER TONAL PALETTE\n\nMove away from using near-black as the dominant background across the entire page.\n\nUse a sophisticated range of:\n\n* Deep charcoal\n* Graphite\n* Warm black\n* Gunmetal\n* Soft stone\n* Muted silver\n* Warm off-white\n* Subtle champagne/bronze highlights where appropriate\n\nThe page should have visible tonal variation between sections.\n\nUse lighter surfaces strategically so the website can breathe.\n\n### 2. USE LIGHT AND SHADOW LIKE A LUXURY BRAND\n\nInstead of making everything darker to create contrast, create contrast through:\n\n* Photography\n* Natural shadows\n* Soft highlights\n* Light typography\n* Spacious layouts\n* Tonal transitions\n* Subtle texture\n* Carefully controlled gradients\n\nThe website should feel **expensive**, not gloomy.\n\n### 3. INTRODUCE LIGHT SECTIONS\n\nCreate a deliberate rhythm between dark and light sections.\n\nFor example:\n\nHERO\n\342\206\222 cinematic dark production environment\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\342\206\222 warm off-white / refined light surface\n\nCAPABILITIES\n\342\206\222 graphite\n\nHOW WE WORK\n\342\206\222 warm light / stone surface\n\nPRODUCTION IN ACTION\n\342\206\222 cinematic photography\n\nGLOBAL PRODUCTION\n\342\206\222 deep charcoal\n\nRECENT PRODUCTION WORK\n\342\206\222 refined light or graphite\n\nFINAL CTA\n\342\206\222 cinematic dark photography\n\nThis creates visual pacing and prevents the page from becoming one continuous black wall.\n\n### 4. PHOTOGRAPHY SHOULD HAVE MORE LIGHT\n\nDo not bury the broadcast photography beneath heavy black overlays.\n\nAllow:\n\n* Camera highlights\n* LED walls\n* Stage lighting\n* Production monitors\n* Faces and operators\n* Metallic equipment\n* Architectural details\n\nto remain visible.\n\nUse overlays only where necessary to protect text readability.\n\nThe photography should feel **cinematic and premium**, not artificially darkened.\n\n### 5. LUXURY EDITORIAL DIRECTION\n\nThink more like a high-end international production company, creative studio or luxury editorial publication.\n\nUse:\n\n* Large confident typography\n* Generous whitespace\n* Strong grid systems\n* Elegant image cropping\n* Fine borders\n* Small technical labels\n* Restrained uppercase typography\n* Sophisticated spacing\n* Subtle transitions between materials and tones\n\nAvoid trying to make the website look \342\200\234luxury\342\200\235 through gold gradients, excessive shine or obvious luxury clich\303\251s.\n\n### 6. COLOR ACCENTS\n\nKeep the overall palette restrained.\n\nIf an accent is needed, use it sparingly:\n\n* Warm white\n* Soft silver\n* Very subtle champagne\n* Muted metallic tones\n\nDo NOT introduce:\n\n* Neon blue\n* Purple gradients\n* Cyberpunk colors\n* Excessive gold\n* Glowing effects\n\nThe D1 Streams identity should remain sophisticated and contemporary.\n\n### 7. BACKGROUND TEXTURE\n\nAdd very subtle visual texture where appropriate:\n\n* Fine film grain\n* Natural photographic texture\n* Soft tonal gradients\n* Subtle paper/editorial texture on light sections\n\nKeep these extremely restrained.\n\nThe background should have **depth**, not visual noise.\n\n### 8. TYPOGRAPHY\n\nKeep the existing:\n\n**Syne** \342\200\224 major headlines\n**Plus Jakarta Sans** \342\200\224 body/interface\n**JetBrains Mono** \342\200\224 technical metadata\n\nHowever, make better use of the typography against the new tonal system.\n\nLarge headlines should have room to breathe.\n\nDo not place every headline over an extremely dark background.\n\n### 9. THE FINAL TEST\n\nLook at the entire website as one continuous composition.\n\nIt should feel like:\n\n**A premium international broadcast production company with a sophisticated editorial identity.**\n\nIt should NOT feel like:\n\n**A generic dark-mode technology startup.**\n\nThe viewer should experience:\n\n**LIGHT \342\206\222 SHADOW \342\206\222 IMAGE \342\206\222 TYPOGRAPHY \342\206\222 SPACE \342\206\222 LIGHT**\n\nrather than:\n\n**BLACK \342\206\222 BLACK \342\206\222 BLACK \342\206\222 BLACK**\n\nKeep the cinematic broadcast photography, responsive behavior, performance optimization, contact functionality, recent-production section and plain-text NAMACO credit exactly as currently implemented.\n\nThis is a **visual refinement**, not a complete redesign."
    items {
      text: "## VISUAL REFINEMENT \342\200\224 MAKE THE WEBSITE LUXURIOUS, NOT TOO DARK\n\nThe current background treatment is too dark and is beginning to feel flat, heavy and slightly boring.\n\nDo NOT abandon the cinematic broadcast direction. Instead, elevate it into a **luxury international media-production aesthetic**.\n\nThe goal is:\n\n**CINEMATIC + LUXURIOUS + EDITORIAL + PROFESSIONAL + TECHNICAL**\n\nNot:\n\n**BLACK + DARK + BLACK + DARK**\n\n### 1. CREATE A RICHER TONAL PALETTE\n\nMove away from using near-black as the dominant background across the entire page.\n\nUse a sophisticated range of:\n\n* Deep charcoal\n* Graphite\n* Warm black\n* Gunmetal\n* Soft stone\n* Muted silver\n* Warm off-white\n* Subtle champagne/bronze highlights where appropriate\n\nThe page should have visible tonal variation between sections.\n\nUse lighter surfaces strategically so the website can breathe.\n\n### 2. USE LIGHT AND SHADOW LIKE A LUXURY BRAND\n\nInstead of making everything darker to create contrast, create contrast through:\n\n* Photography\n* Natural shadows\n* Soft highlights\n* Light typography\n* Spacious layouts\n* Tonal transitions\n* Subtle texture\n* Carefully controlled gradients\n\nThe website should feel **expensive**, not gloomy.\n\n### 3. INTRODUCE LIGHT SECTIONS\n\nCreate a deliberate rhythm between dark and light sections.\n\nFor example:\n\nHERO\n\342\206\222 cinematic dark production environment\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\342\206\222 warm off-white / refined light surface\n\nCAPABILITIES\n\342\206\222 graphite\n\nHOW WE WORK\n\342\206\222 warm light / stone surface\n\nPRODUCTION IN ACTION\n\342\206\222 cinematic photography\n\nGLOBAL PRODUCTION\n\342\206\222 deep charcoal\n\nRECENT PRODUCTION WORK\n\342\206\222 refined light or graphite\n\nFINAL CTA\n\342\206\222 cinematic dark photography\n\nThis creates visual pacing and prevents the page from becoming one continuous black wall.\n\n### 4. PHOTOGRAPHY SHOULD HAVE MORE LIGHT\n\nDo not bury the broadcast photography beneath heavy black overlays.\n\nAllow:\n\n* Camera highlights\n* LED walls\n* Stage lighting\n* Production monitors\n* Faces and operators\n* Metallic equipment\n* Architectural details\n\nto remain visible.\n\nUse overlays only where necessary to protect text readability.\n\nThe photography should feel **cinematic and premium**, not artificially darkened.\n\n### 5. LUXURY EDITORIAL DIRECTION\n\nThink more like a high-end international production company, creative studio or luxury editorial publication.\n\nUse:\n\n* Large confident typography\n* Generous whitespace\n* Strong grid systems\n* Elegant image cropping\n* Fine borders\n* Small technical labels\n* Restrained uppercase typography\n* Sophisticated spacing\n* Subtle transitions between materials and tones\n\nAvoid trying to make the website look \342\200\234luxury\342\200\235 through gold gradients, excessive shine or obvious luxury clich\303\251s.\n\n### 6. COLOR ACCENTS\n\nKeep the overall palette restrained.\n\nIf an accent is needed, use it sparingly:\n\n* Warm white\n* Soft silver\n* Very subtle champagne\n* Muted metallic tones\n\nDo NOT introduce:\n\n* Neon blue\n* Purple gradients\n* Cyberpunk colors\n* Excessive gold\n* Glowing effects\n\nThe D1 Streams identity should remain sophisticated and contemporary.\n\n### 7. BACKGROUND TEXTURE\n\nAdd very subtle visual texture where appropriate:\n\n* Fine film grain\n* Natural photographic texture\n* Soft tonal gradients\n* Subtle paper/editorial texture on light sections\n\nKeep these extremely restrained.\n\nThe background should have **depth**, not visual noise.\n\n### 8. TYPOGRAPHY\n\nKeep the existing:\n\n**Syne** \342\200\224 major headlines\n**Plus Jakarta Sans** \342\200\224 body/interface\n**JetBrains Mono** \342\200\224 technical metadata\n\nHowever, make better use of the typography against the new tonal system.\n\nLarge headlines should have room to breathe.\n\nDo not place every headline over an extremely dark background.\n\n### 9. THE FINAL TEST\n\nLook at the entire website as one continuous composition.\n\nIt should feel like:\n\n**A premium international broadcast production company with a sophisticated editorial identity.**\n\nIt should NOT feel like:\n\n**A generic dark-mode technology startup.**\n\nThe viewer should experience:\n\n**LIGHT \342\206\222 SHADOW \342\206\222 IMAGE \342\206\222 TYPOGRAPHY \342\206\222 SPACE \342\206\222 LIGHT**\n\nrather than:\n\n**BLACK \342\206\222 BLACK \342\206\222 BLACK \342\206\222 BLACK**\n\nKeep the cinematic broadcast photography, responsive behavior, performance optimization, contact functionality, recent-production section and plain-text NAMACO credit exactly as currently implemented.\n\nThis is a **visual refinement**, not a complete redesign."
    }
